' ARCADE -- Arco Runtime Construction & Application Development Environment.
'
' Phase B (packet Section 34): the minimum shell that can open a project and show four tabs
' (Surface / Flow / Source / Run) sharing one project context. No visual design canvas yet -- per
' the packet's own acceptance criterion, Source is the only functional tab; Surface/Flow/Run show
' an honest "not yet implemented" placeholder rather than a fake preview (packet Section 39.4/39.8
' forbid faking a renderer or hot-reload theater; the same "don't pretend" discipline applies here
' to an empty tab).
'
' See docs/arcade/README.md and docs/ARCADE_PROGRESS.md for the architecture this implements and
' the reasoning behind each decision below.

#IMPORT "arcoui"

' The ARCADE project file format is `.arcoproj` -- a plain ArcoBASIC object-literal expression,
' already real and already loadable via Project.Load (see docs/arcade/README.md's Session 1
' finding 8). Recognized fields: Name, Entry, Files, Window. This loading logic mirrors the
' now-deleted arcoflow.abas prototype's own (proven, working) version of it -- see
' docs/ARCADE_PROGRESS.md for why that prototype was removed; this loading logic specifically was
' worth keeping, since the format itself is an existing convention, not something ARCADE invented.
FUNCTION LoadProject(openedPath)
    project = {}
    project.Name = ""
    project.Root = ""
    project.Entry = ""
    project.Files = []

    projectPath = ""
    IF openedPath <> "" ANDALSO Path.Extension(openedPath) == ".arcoproj" THEN
        projectPath = openedPath
    ELSE
        candidate = Path.Join(Path.DirName(openedPath), "project.arcoproj")
        IF File.Exists(candidate) THEN projectPath = candidate
    END IF

    IF projectPath <> "" THEN
        TRY
            loaded = Project.Load(projectPath)
            project.Name = Object.Get(loaded, "Name", "")
            project.Entry = Object.Get(loaded, "Entry", "")
            project.Root = Path.DirName(projectPath)
            project.Files = Object.Get(loaded, "Files", [])
            ' Deliberately NOT reading the project's own `Window` field here -- that config
            ' describes the size of the WINDOW THE OPENED PROJECT ITSELF WOULD OPEN AT RUNTIME
            ' (see arcade/reference-project/project.arcoproj's own 420x220, sized for Customer
            ' Lookup's own small window, found the hard way: reading it here made ARCADE's OWN
            ' shell open at 420x220 too, cramped enough to clip the fourth tab). ARCADE's own
            ' workspace window size is a separate, ARCADE-level concern -- see WindowWidth/
            ' WindowHeight constants below.
        CATCH err
            PRINT "ARCADE could not load " + projectPath + ": " + err.Message
        END TRY
    END IF

    RETURN project
END FUNCTION

' The four canonical views (packet Section 4). "RUN" stays last in both the tab order and this
' list on purpose -- it is the odd one out (it doesn't show a static representation of the project
' at all, it launches the real thing), matching the packet's own workspace mockup ordering
' (Section 33: "SURFACE FLOW SOURCE RUN").
FUNCTION ViewNames()
    RETURN ["Surface", "Flow", "Source", "Run"]
END FUNCTION

' The content area beneath the tab bar: shows the Source editor when active, an honest "not yet
' implemented" notice for Surface/Flow otherwise, and launches the project's own entry file for
' Run. A single Widget rather than four separately-attached ones because only one is ever visible
' at a time and they share the same bounds -- ArcoSurface has no concept of "hidden" widgets, so
' the alternative (attaching all four and toggling visibility by hand in each Draw/HandleEvent)
' would just move this same branching into ArcadeShell instead of removing it.
CLASS ContentArea IMPLEMENTS Widget
    ActiveTab = "Source"
    SourceEditor = NULL
    EntryPath = ""
    ProjectName = ""
    X = 0
    Y = 0
    Width = 0
    Height = 0
    RunStatus = ""
    RunInFlight = FALSE
    RunOutputPath = ""

    CONSTRUCTOR(entryPath, projectName)
        SELF.EntryPath = entryPath
        SELF.ProjectName = projectName
        content = ""
        IF entryPath <> "" ANDALSO File.Exists(entryPath) THEN content = File.ReadText(entryPath)
        SELF.SourceEditor = TextArea(content)
    END CONSTRUCTOR

    FUNCTION SetBounds(x, y, width, height)
        SELF.X = x
        SELF.Y = y
        SELF.Width = width
        SELF.Height = height
        ignored = SELF.SourceEditor.SetBounds(x, y, width, height)
    END FUNCTION

    ' Writes the Source editor's current text back to the entry file. Phase B's acceptance is
    ' "source file can be opened and edited" -- this is the "and saved" half, bound to Ctrl+S
    ' below, the conventional editor shortcut rather than an ARCADE invention.
    FUNCTION SaveSource()
        IF SELF.EntryPath == "" THEN RETURN FALSE
        File.WriteText(SELF.EntryPath, SELF.SourceEditor.Text)
        RETURN TRUE
    END FUNCTION

    FUNCTION RunProject()
        IF SELF.EntryPath == "" THEN
            SELF.RunStatus = "No entry file to run."
            RETURN
        END IF
        IF SELF.RunInFlight THEN
            SELF.RunStatus = "A run is already in progress -- wait for it to finish, or close its window."
            RETURN
        END IF
        ' Compiled-and-run each time, not "launch a pre-built binary": Run must reflect whatever
        ' is actually on disk right now, including an unsaved-to-Source-but-saved-to-disk edit --
        ' see docs/arcade/runtime-inspection.md's own "no fake hot reload" discipline. This shells
        ' out the same way the deleted arcoflow.abas prototype did (Process.Run -> ArcoFission
        ' compile-run), the one piece of its approach worth keeping as-is.
        '
        ' What changed, and why (a real, live-reported bug): Process.Run is a genuinely BLOCKING
        ' call (popen()/pclose() in src/runtime/runtime.cpp's process_run) -- it does not return
        ' until the child process exits. Customer Lookup (like any ArcoUI app) opens its own real
        ' GUI window and keeps running until a person closes THAT window. A synchronous Process.Run
        ' here therefore froze ARCADE's ENTIRE event loop -- no redraws, no click handling, not even
        ' ARCADE's own close button -- for as long as the run stayed open. Confirmed live: pressing
        ' R made the whole shell unresponsive, reported as "it doesn't let me close the windows...
        ' nothing." The fix backgrounds the child (a trailing `&` inside a subshell, still launched
        ' through the same Process.Run/popen path -- the outer shell backgrounds the subshell and
        ' returns immediately, so Process.Run itself no longer blocks) and appends a sentinel line
        ' once it truly exits, so ARCADE stays fully responsive while a run is open. PollRun() below
        ' is the other half: it notices the sentinel and reports the real captured output once the
        ' child actually finishes, so this stays honest -- real output, just observed
        ' asynchronously -- rather than a fire-and-forget approximation that would silently swallow
        ' compile errors (this Run tab's other job).
        arcofission = Process.Env("ARCOFISSION_PATH")
        IF arcofission == "" THEN arcofission = "ArcoFission"
        SELF.RunOutputPath = "/tmp/arcade-run-" + Time.Timestamp() + ".log"
        ' The redirect MUST be on the outer subshell, not the inner command -- found the hard way,
        ' by measuring it. Process.Run is popen()/pclose() (src/runtime/runtime.cpp's process_run),
        ' and popen's read loop blocks until the pipe sees EOF, which does NOT happen just because
        ' the direct `sh -c` child has exited: a backgrounded grandchild that inherited a copy of
        ' the pipe's write end before performing its OWN redirection keeps that fd alive until IT
        ' exits. `(cmd > file 2>&1; ...) &` still measured ~4s for a 4s inner sleep -- the redirect
        ' happening one level too deep. Moving the redirect onto the whole backgrounded subshell,
        ' `(cmd; ...) > file 2>&1 &`, closes the pipe's write end immediately at subshell start,
        ' before anything inside it runs -- measured ~1ms for the same 4s inner sleep. This was the
        ' actual, full reason ARCADE stayed frozen even after the first attempt at this fix.
        command = "(" + ShellQuoteArcade(arcofission) + " compile-run " + ShellQuoteArcade(SELF.EntryPath) + "; echo ARCADE_RUN_DONE) > " + ShellQuoteArcade(SELF.RunOutputPath) + " 2>&1 &"
        ignored = Process.Run(command)
        SELF.RunInFlight = TRUE
        SELF.RunStatus = "Running..."
    END FUNCTION

    ' Cheap (one File.Exists, and only a File.ReadText once that returns TRUE) and a complete no-op
    ' whenever no run is in flight -- safe to call every main-loop iteration. Returns TRUE the one
    ' time it notices a run just finished (so the caller knows to force a redraw of the new status).
    '
    ' `!`, not `NOT` -- a real bug, found by direct measurement while debugging why this function
    ' never seemed to fire: NOT/BITNOT in this language is a bitwise complement (~), aliased at the
    ' lexer level (src/frontend/lexer.cpp), not logical negation. `NOT TRUE` measures as -2, which
    ' is itself truthy, so `IF NOT x THEN` was taking its branch regardless of x's real value --
    ' every guard below always returned early. See stdlib/arcoui.abas's TextField.HandleEvent and
    ' App.AnyOpen for the same fix applied to the wider bug this one led to being found.
    FUNCTION PollRun()
        IF !SELF.RunInFlight THEN RETURN FALSE
        IF !File.Exists(SELF.RunOutputPath) THEN RETURN FALSE
        text = File.ReadText(SELF.RunOutputPath)
        marker = "ARCADE_RUN_DONE"
        IF !String.Contains(text, marker) THEN RETURN FALSE
        SELF.RunStatus = String.Trim(String.Replace(text, marker, ""))
        SELF.RunInFlight = FALSE
        RETURN TRUE
    END FUNCTION

    FUNCTION Draw(window)
        IF SELF.ActiveTab == "Source" THEN
            SELF.SourceEditor.Focused = TRUE
            ignored = SELF.SourceEditor.Draw(window)
        ELSE
            SELF.SourceEditor.Focused = FALSE
            IF SELF.ActiveTab == "Run" THEN
                GUI.Text(window, "Run -- " + SELF.EntryPath, SELF.X, SELF.Y, 16, 0.82, 0.86, 0.90, 1.0)
                GUI.Text(window, "Press R to compile and run through ArcoFission compile-run.", SELF.X, SELF.Y + 26, 13, 0.56, 0.64, 0.66, 1.0)
                IF SELF.RunStatus <> "" THEN
                    GUI.Text(window, SELF.RunStatus, SELF.X, SELF.Y + 56, 13, 0.7, 0.85, 0.75, 1.0)
                END IF
            ELSE
                GUI.Text(window, SELF.ActiveTab + " -- not yet implemented.", SELF.X, SELF.Y, 16, 0.6, 0.65, 0.7, 1.0)
                GUI.Text(window, "See docs/arcade/" + Lower(SELF.ActiveTab) + ".md for direction.", SELF.X, SELF.Y + 26, 13, 0.5, 0.55, 0.58, 1.0)
            END IF
        END IF
    END FUNCTION

    FUNCTION HandleEvent(window, event)
        changed = FALSE
        IF SELF.ActiveTab == "Source" THEN
            changed = SELF.SourceEditor.HandleEvent(window, event)
            IF event.Type == "key" ANDALSO event.Action == "press" ANDALSO event.Ctrl ANDALSO event.Key == "s" THEN
                ignored = SELF.SaveSource()
            END IF
        END IF
        IF SELF.ActiveTab == "Run" ANDALSO event.Type == "key" ANDALSO event.Action == "press" ANDALSO event.Key == "r" THEN
            ignored = SELF.SaveSource()
            ignored = SELF.RunProject()
        END IF
        RETURN changed
    END FUNCTION
END CLASS

FUNCTION ShellQuoteArcade(text)
    RETURN "'" + String.Replace(text, "'", "'\''") + "'"
END FUNCTION

IF GUI.Available() == FALSE THEN
    PRINT "ARCADE needs a Wayland or X11 desktop session."
ELSE
    TRY
        openedPath = ""
        IF LEN(Args) > 0 THEN openedPath = Args[0]

        project = LoadProject(openedPath)
        entryPath = ""
        IF project.Entry <> "" THEN entryPath = Path.Join(project.Root, project.Entry)

        windowTitle = "ARCADE"
        IF project.Name <> "" THEN windowTitle = "ARCADE -- " + project.Name

        ' ARCADE's own workspace window size -- deliberately independent of whatever window size
        ' the opened project's own app declares for itself (see LoadProject's own comment).
        shellWidth = 1200
        shellHeight = 760

        app = App("ARCADE")
        win = app.Window(windowTitle, shellWidth, shellHeight)

        views = ViewNames()
        content = ContentArea(entryPath, project.Name)

        ' One intent per tab, one Button per tab -- reusing the same, already-proven Button/Widget/
        ' Intent machinery ArcoUI's own examples use for every other click target, rather than
        ' hand-rolling tab-bar hit-testing inside ArcadeShell (packet Section 39.6 forbids a
        ' Surface-specific event system separate from what ArcoBASIC/ArcoUI already provides; the
        ' same discipline applies to ARCADE's own UI, not just to applications ARCADE edits).
        '
        ' Kept in `tabButtons` (parallel to `views`), not just attached and forgotten: the
        ' keyboard-FOCUS ring ArcoSurface draws around whichever widget was attached first
        ' (Surface, always, regardless of which tab is actually showing -- Tab/Shift+Tab moves it,
        ' a click does NOT) is a completely different concept from "which tab is active," and
        ' conflating them was a real, found-by-a-real-user bug: Surface stayed visually
        ' highlighted no matter which tab's content was actually showing. Fixed by giving the
        ' ACTIVE tab its own real color state, applied every frame in the main loop below, the
        ' same way Arconaut's own Tab() (arcfs-utils/apps/arconaut/arconaut.abas) already
        ' distinguishes a selected tab from an unselected one via explicit color, not the
        ' keyboard-focus ring.
        tabButtons = []
        tabX = win.Theme.Spacing
        FOR viewName IN views
            ignored = win.DefineIntent("Show" + viewName)
            tabButton = Button(viewName)
            tabButton.SetBounds(tabX, win.Theme.Spacing, 110, 34)
            ignored = win.Attach(tabButton, "Show" + viewName)
            ignored = Array.Push(tabButtons, tabButton)
            tabX = tabX + 110 + win.Theme.Spacing
        NEXT

        contentY = win.Theme.Spacing * 2 + 34
        ignored = content.SetBounds(win.Theme.Spacing, contentY, shellWidth - win.Theme.Spacing * 2, shellHeight - contentY - win.Theme.Spacing)
        ignored = win.Attach(content, "", TRUE)

        ' Redraw gating (packet Section 38: "avoid architectures that require reparsing/rebuilding
        ' the entire project after every mouse movement" -- the same principle applies to redrawing
        ' it). ArcoUI's own App.Start() redraws unconditionally every loop iteration, "matching
        ' every existing GUI.* example app's convention" per its own comment -- fine for a small,
        ' cheap-to-draw app, but ARCADE's own Source tab draws real, potentially long file content
        ' every frame, and a single mouse click is at least two GUI.WaitEvent iterations
        ' (pointer-button press, then release -- Button.HandleEvent only activates on release, see
        ' stdlib/gui.abas) before the resulting Show* intent even reaches PollEvent() below, plus
        ' whatever pointer-move events land in between -- every one of which triggered a full,
        ' otherwise-wasted redraw before this fix, a real, reported "slow to update after clicking
        ' a button" symptom. `needsRedraw` tracks whether anything actually changed since the last
        ' draw; a small periodic safety-net redraw (every ~10 idle iterations, ~200ms at this
        ' loop's own 0.02s poll interval) guards against any repaint this change-tracking doesn't
        ' account for (e.g. window expose after being uncovered) rather than trusting the tracking
        ' to be exhaustive.
        needsRedraw = TRUE
        idleTicks = 0
        WHILE app.AnyOpen()
            timestamp = Time.Timestamp()
            event = GUI.WaitEvent(0.02)
            IF event.Type <> "none" THEN
                needsRedraw = TRUE
                target = app.SurfaceForWindow(event.Window)
                IF target <> NULL THEN
                    IF event.Type == "close" THEN
                        ignored = target.Invoke("Close", timestamp)
                    ELSE
                        ignored = target.HandleEvent(event, timestamp)
                    END IF
                END IF
                ' Explicit quit affordance (packet Section 27: keyboard access, not just mouse/
                ' drag-and-drop). A real, reported bug: this environment's window manager adds NO
                ' visible title bar/close button around an ordinary (non-shaped) ArcoUI window --
                ' confirmed via `xwininfo -tree`, the WM's own decoration frame is the exact same
                ' size as the content, zero chrome added -- so there was LITERALLY no way to close
                ' ARCADE at all before this fix. A real window-manager-level close request (Alt+F4,
                ' a taskbar close, `xdotool windowclose`) already worked correctly (confirmed
                ' separately) via the ordinary event.Type == "close" path above; this adds the
                ' missing IN-APPLICATION affordance, Ctrl+Q, the conventional cross-platform "quit"
                ' shortcut (Escape is deliberately NOT used for this -- it's a reasonable future
                ' "cancel the current operation" key inside an editor, and overloading it to also
                ' mean "quit the whole application" would be a worse trap than the one being fixed).
                IF event.Type == "key" ANDALSO event.Action == "press" ANDALSO event.Ctrl ANDALSO event.Key == "q" THEN
                    FOR surface IN app.Surfaces
                        ignored = surface.Invoke("Close", timestamp)
                    NEXT
                END IF
            END IF
            ignored = app.Pump()

            tabEvent = app.PollEvent()
            WHILE tabEvent <> NULL
                needsRedraw = TRUE
                FOR viewName IN views
                    IF tabEvent.Intent == win.IntentHandle("Show" + viewName) THEN content.ActiveTab = viewName
                NEXT
                tabEvent = app.PollEvent()
            WEND

            IF content.PollRun() THEN needsRedraw = TRUE

            idleTicks = idleTicks + 1
            IF idleTicks > 10 THEN
                needsRedraw = TRUE
                idleTicks = 0
            END IF

            IF needsRedraw THEN
                ' Active-tab color, applied fresh every draw (cheap -- four field assignments, no
                ' allocation) rather than only on a tab switch, so it stays correct even if a
                ' button's own color fields were ever touched some other way in the future.
                i = 0
                FOR viewName IN views
                    tabButton = tabButtons[i]
                    IF viewName == content.ActiveTab THEN
                        tabButton.BackgroundR = win.Theme.AccentR
                        tabButton.BackgroundG = win.Theme.AccentG
                        tabButton.BackgroundB = win.Theme.AccentB
                    ELSE
                        tabButton.BackgroundR = 0.14
                        tabButton.BackgroundG = 0.18
                        tabButton.BackgroundB = 0.25
                    END IF
                    i = i + 1
                NEXT
                FOR surface IN app.Surfaces
                    IF !surface.ShouldClose() THEN ignored = surface.Draw()
                NEXT
                needsRedraw = FALSE
            END IF
        WEND
        FOR surface IN app.Surfaces
            ignored = surface.Close()
        NEXT
    CATCH err
        PRINT "ARCADE failed: " + err
    END TRY
END IF
